  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 326 AM CDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\nChallenges: Lingering rain chances through the day with residual \ncloud cover and its impact on temperatures\n\nThe bulk of the precipitation overnight has been over northern \nKansas which has mainly shifted into the northeast quadrant. \nActivity to the south across southern Kansas has diminished pretty \nsteadily with light accumulations if any for locations lucky enough \nto get under a shower. Precipitation should continue to push east \nthrough sunrise and potentially lingering into mid day in southeast \nKansas as a subtle shortwave tracks southeast through the Central \nPlains. A brief period of heavy rain and possibly lightning would be \nthe main hazards albeit if more robust activity stalls this could \nlead to additional localized flooding concerns. The respective \nlongevity could keep temperatures lower especially in southeast \nKansas similar to what occurred on Thursday which is why \ntemperatures were dropped a few degrees from the NBM. Next the \nquestion is how quickly will the residual cloud cover clear out of \nsouth central Kansas today and what respective impact will that have \non temperatures as well as heat indices. While the current forecast \nis for heat indices to reach around 105 specifically in Harper \nCounty, it was decided to hold off on any headlines given the \nuncertainty.   \n\nPrecipitation chances (20-30%) were kept in for southeast Kansas \nthrough the evening hours, but the latest short term guidance \nsuggests showers and storms should exit prior to 0Z/7PM with no \nadditional development anticipated. There may be adjustments to \nremove these chances even quicker if this trend persists. The mid \nand upper ridge axis over the Mountain West/western CONUS pushes \neastward into the central CONUS for the weekend resulting in a \nreturn of the heat. After a couple of days below seasonal normal \ntemperatures, the streak will end with values closer to normal \n(lower 90s) today for all but mainly southeast Kansas. This is just \na warm up for what the weekend into next week will bring with values \naround 100-105 degrees area wide on Sunday and Monday. Heat indices \nof 105 to 112 are forecast from Saturday to Tuesday. Wet bulb globe \ntemperatures (WBGT) are in the highest category for this timeframe \nin many locations as well. HeatRisk indicates the major category \nwhich would affect anyone without cooling/hydration as well as \nhealth systems and industries; given the time of year, the \nprobabilistic values for reaching extreme are primarily below 50%. \nSince the heat really starts Saturday afternoon, the decision was to \nallow the next shift to issue the headline(s). As a side climate \nnote, overnight temperatures into early next week are forecast near \nrecord warm low values. \n\nThere are subtle short waves through the Plains late Sunday into \nMonday and again mid week. It is possible that this could bring \nprecipitation chances especially in northern Kansas, but the current \nNBM forecast is around or below 20% for any rain chances for the \nrest of the period. A low pressure system tracks across central \nCanada on Wednesday reducing the amplification of the ridge and \nshifting it westward as the system deepens over Hudson Bay. This \ncould set up a trough east of the Mississippi River for the late \nparts of next week into the weekend. If so, this could tame \ntemperatures at least slightly.",
